  <HOMEWORK title="Bring-Up 6: The last tap, still on its coarse setting"
            prompt="CDR locked, CTLE boost set. Bring up the DFE last - it needs correct decisions from an already-locked, already-equalized receiver, which is exactly why it comes after CDR and CTLE in this plan, not before. Auto-adapt is on."
            hint="A short BER check here might come back completely clean either way. Look at the eye's height and width readouts instead of just the error count - do they look as good as they could?"
            explanation="The DFE's adaptation step is left at 0.01, its coarsest setting - good for grabbing a rough tap value fast during an initial sweep, too coarse for settled, steady-state operation: the taps keep hunting back and forth around the right value instead of settling onto it. A short BER run can come back with zero errors either way (there usually isn't enough margin lost to flip a bit most of the time), which is exactly why this step matters: eye height comes in around 55% here, versus about 66% once the step is tightened to a normal 0.001 (see Bring-Up 9's otherwise-identical configuration) - real margin given up to a setting that's easy to forget to tighten after bring-up's first, coarse pass. This is the same lesson Bring-Up 9 ends on: a clean BER count over a short run doesn't mean there's nothing left to improve, and eye margin is a more sensitive instrument to check than a short error count alone."/>
</LABCHAIN>
